  title="Planning a Family Around a Rotating Schedule"
  description="The systems shift-work families actually use: the shared calendar, holiday trade season, aiming leave at natural breaks, childcare on drifting cycles, and two-rotation households."
  h1="Family Planning on a Rotating Schedule"
>
  <p>
    The hardest part of shift work is rarely the shifts — it is that the rest of the family lives
    on a seven-day week and you don't. Weddings get scheduled, playoffs get scheduled, school
    plays get scheduled, all by people who assume weekends are free. The families that make
    rotations work don't have easier schedules; they have <strong>systems</strong>. These are the
    ones that come up again and again.
  </p>

  <h2>One shared calendar, twelve months deep</h2>
  <p>
    The single highest-leverage move: your rotation lives in the <em>family's</em> calendar, not in
    your head. A rotation is deterministic — one known workday defines the entire year — so
    generate the full 12 months and <a href="/">export it to the shared phone calendar</a> once.
    Now your partner answers "can we do the 14th?" without texting you, invitations get RSVP'd in
    thirty seconds, and the recurring argument about who forgot to mention a shift disappears. Tape
    the <a href="/2-2-3-panama-schedule-calculator">printed wall calendar</a> to the fridge for the
    kids; the phone version for the adults.
  </p>

  <h2>Holiday trade season is a calendar event</h2>
  <p>
    Holiday luck rotates — the cycle that worked your Christmas this year probably frees it next
    year. Check <a href="/am-i-working-on-christmas">Christmas</a> and
    <a href="/am-i-working-on-thanksgiving">Thanksgiving</a> the moment the year's calendar exists,
    then decide as a family which dates are worth trading for. The market matters: Thanksgiving
    swaps clear around Labor Day, Christmas swaps by early fall, and the cross-holiday deal — your
    Thanksgiving for their Christmas — is the best trade in shift work when the cycle assigns you
    both. Waiting until November is how you end up owing double favors.
  </p>

  <h2>Spend leave where the cycle is generous</h2>
  <p>
    Vacation days next to natural breaks buy disproportionate time: one leave day attached to a
    2-2-3's three-day weekend makes five days; two attached to a
    <a href="/4-on-4-off-schedule-calculator">4-on-4-off</a> break makes eight. DuPont families
    barely use leave for trips at all — the monthly seven-day break carries them. The wrong move is
    burning leave to fix a badly-placed week when a well-placed request one cycle later would have
    cost half as much.
  </p>

  <h2>Childcare on a drifting cycle</h2>
  <p>
    Fixed-anchor patterns (2-2-3, Pitman) fit daycare contracts because your weekdays never move.
    Drifting patterns need different structures: flexible or drop-in care, a rotating grandparent
    day pinned to cycle-days rather than weekdays, or — the two-shift-household classic — partners
    on opposite squads covering each other, with the overlap days visible a year out on the shared
    calendar. Whichever structure, the enabling condition is the same: everyone involved can see
    the pattern months ahead.
  </p>

  <h2>Protect the reunion, not just the schedule</h2>
  <p>
    Veteran shift families warn about the same trap: treating every off-day as catch-up
    (sleep, errands, repairs) until family time is whatever's left. The fix is naming days.
    First day after nights is recovery — expect a zombie, plan nothing. One day per break is a
    family day that outranks errands. School events that matter get flagged the moment the
    calendar is generated, so a trade or leave request goes in months early, not the week of. A
    rotation takes some weekends; the system's job is making sure it never silently takes the ones
    that mattered.
  </p>
